  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Unseasonable warmth through the rest of the week, with \n   scattered afternoon thunderstorms in Southwest Montana and \n   Lemhi County today.\n\n-  Cold front Friday brings widespread gusty winds, transitioning\n   to a much cooler, breezy, and showery weekend.\n\nUnseasonably warm and mostly dry conditions will persist across \nthe Northern Rockies today, with valley temperatures running 10 to\n15 degrees above seasonal averages. An upper-level shortwave \nlifting northeastward will interact with monsoonal moisture to \ntrigger scattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms between 2 PM\nand 10 PM MDT. Convective activity will be focused across Lemhi \nCounty, the southern Bitterroot, and Southwest Montana, with a \nvery low probability (10%) of showers reaching the Mission and \nFlathead valleys. Primary storm hazards include erratic outflow \nwind gusts of 30 to 40 mph (less than 10% chance for isolated \ngusts to 45 mph), lightning, and brief localized heavy rainfall.\n\nWarm and mostly dry conditions will persist Wednesday into \nThursday under southwesterly flow aloft. A cold front will pass \nthrough the region on Friday, bringing widespread gusty winds and\nconcerns for choppy lakes. While this could produce elevated fire\nweather conditions for active fire and prescribed burning, \nrelative humidity and fuel moisture parameters are not expected \nto produce sustained nor widespread critical Red Flag conditions \nand criteria at this time.\n\nA significant change arrives this weekend as an upper- level \ncutoff low tracks into the region. Guidance continues to favor a \nmore northerly track, focusing the most widespread rainfall across\nNorthwest Montana and the Idaho Panhandle. Snow levels lower to \naround 6,000 feet near the center of the low's track, introducing \nwinter- like backcountry impacts to the higher terrain, \nparticularly Glacier National Park. Seasonably cool temperatures \nwill follow, bringing clearing skies and frosty mornings early \nnext week.",
